{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-refactor` skill MUST be the home of moving code, tests, and requirements to another file, chapter, or outline node, and of renaming requirements.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has two obligations: `habeas-refactor` is the home of moving code, tests and requirements to another file, chapter or outline node, and it is the home of renaming requirements. Both are in the scope of the parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure` rule that a topic is stated in full in exactly one skill. `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_is_home_of_moves_and_requirement_renames]` covers them.\n\n- **Moves.** The test reads the `## Workflow` section of the `habeas-refactor` `SKILL.md`. It asserts that the section says to move the `{req}` directive to its new chapter. It also asserts the three `--map` forms: a file or directory (`src/config.rs=src/settings.rs`), a node, and a definition. Together these cover moves to another file, chapter or outline node.\n- **Renames.** The test asserts that the skill says \"Renaming a requirement's ID is a definition mapping\". It also asserts the definition-mapping example under Workflow.\n- **Exactly one home.** The test calls `assert_home(\"habeas-refactor\")`. That helper asserts the refactor skill contains every marker in `home_markers(\"habeas-refactor\")`. Those markers are the `--map requirements.cli.check=requirements.core.check` form, the reference-rewriting statement, `habeas refactor --dry-run`, the node-mapping form, and the \"Would leave ... unchanged\" explanation. The helper then asserts that no other skill in `SKILLS` contains any of those markers, so the refactor skill does not share them with another skill. A skill other than `habeas-refactor` that also stated how to move or rename in full would most likely repeat these markers and fail the test. Both the \"home\" and the \"exactly one\" parts therefore have a positive and a negative assertion.\n\nThe helpers `skill_md`, `skill_files`, `section`, `assert_text`, `assert_phrases` and `bundled_skill` are reached from the marked test, so they need no markers of their own. I found no plausible violation of the requirement that these assertions would accept.",
  "references": "a54d07ae817e846f35af61d3ee5e062c81c43bc70e6a4a504a69071debfe727e",
